  "notes": "Third-party BatteryLife v12 pickles of the HNEI cells; materials, nominal capacity and voltage limits are read from each pickle and the test conditions from the cell id. All 14 locally present HNEI BatteryLife pickles were checked with SafeUnpickler: the temperature_in_C column contains 7,359,143 NaN values and zero finite cell-temperature measurements. Chamber/filename temperature (25 C) is a testing condition, not a measured cell-temperature channel. Charging profile: the cached BatteryLife README says \"2 different fast-charging protocols\" and the pickles' charge_protocol metadata says 2C, but the cell ids and the waveforms show one 0.5C charge and 1.5C discharge for every cell; the files are followed. The paper (Devie, Baure and Dubarry, Energies 2018, 11(5), 1031; full text read) and the Battery Archive HNEI study summary agree with this record. Cell: LG Chemical ICR18650 C2, 18650, graphite negative electrode, positive electrode a blend of LiCoO2 and LiNi0.4Mn0.2Co0.4O2 (estimated 60% LCO / 40% NMC), nominal capacity 2800 mAh, recommended 3.00-4.30 V (50 mA cut-off current), recommended charge C/2 (maximum 1C) and maximum discharge 2C. Nominal voltage is not stated. Cycling: 15 of a 51-cell batch (this record holds 14 of them) for 1000 cycles at 25 C between 3.0 and 4.3 V, CC-CV charge at C/2, 1.5C discharge, no rests between cycles, a C/25 RPT every 100 cycles and an extended cycle with three 4 h rests every 25 cycles (Arbin tester, Amerex chambers). The charge rate is therefore C/2: the 2C in the pickles' charge_protocol metadata is wrong, as the waveforms already showed. The registry lists NMC only; the paper (SEM/EDX) shows an LCO/NMC composite, which supports this record's LCO-NMC."
